title: Making a platform game
card: Chapter 12 | Making a game | Hop! A platform game, from the first line to the last
say:
  Now let's put it all together, and make a real game: a platform game called Hop.
  A little blue block runs and jumps across floating platforms, picks up every coin, and reaches the flag at the end.
  We'll write it piece by piece, and look closely at how each piece works.
uncard
new: hop.stella
paste:
  whisper: Hop! A small platformer. Left and right run, space jumps.
  whisper: Pick up every coin, then touch the flag at the far end.

  use: window
  use: maths

  a platform has:
    x (a number)
    y (a number)
    width (a number)
    height (a number)
  and the universe said i love you

  a coin has:
    birth (a flat place)
    taken (yes or no)
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 17
zoom: lines 1-17
spot: lines 1-2
say:
  The top of the game says what it is, in whispers. Then use window, for the window to draw in, and use maths, for sine.
spot: lines 7-12
say:
  A platform is a box: where its top left corner is, x across and y down, and how wide and high it is. Every floor, ledge and pillar in the level is one of these.
spot: lines 14-17
say:
  A coin has a birth, which is where it floats, and so it has an existence too. And taken says whether the player has picked it up.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  the world remembers:
    player x (a number)
    player y (a number)
    speed x (a number)
    speed y (a number)
    on ground (yes or no)
    facing (a number)
    platforms (a list of platforms)
    coins (a list of coins)
    score (a number)
    camera (a number)
    ticks (a number)
    won (yes or no)
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 32
zoom: lines 19-32
spot: lines 20-21
say:
  The world remembers everything about the game while it runs. Player x and player y are where the player's top left corner is.
spot: lines 22-23
say:
  Speed x and speed y are how far the player moves each frame: across, and down. These two are the heart of the game. We'll come back to them.
spot: lines 24-25
say:
  On ground says whether the player is standing on something. Facing is one for right, minus one for left, so the eyes look the right way.
spot: lines 26-28
say:
  The platforms, the coins, and the score.
spot: lines 29-31
say:
  Camera is how far the view has scrolled. Ticks counts time, for the coins' bobbing. And won says whether the game is over.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  whisper: ---- making the level

  to platform at, given x (a number) and y (a number) and width (a number) and height (a number), returning a platform:
    conjure a platform as made
    set the x of made to x
    set the y of made to y
    set the width of made to width
    set the height of made to height
    return made
  and the universe said i love you

  to coin at, given x (a number) and y (a number), returning a coin:
    conjure a coin as made
    set the birth of made to x horizontal y vertical
    return made
  and the universe said i love you

  to build the level:
    add platform at with (0, 460, 760, 80) to platforms
    add platform at with (880, 460, 720, 80) to platforms
    add platform at with (1720, 460, 300, 80) to platforms
    add platform at with (2160, 460, 1040, 80) to platforms
    add platform at with (330, 360, 160, 24) to platforms
    add platform at with (1180, 340, 140, 24) to platforms
    add platform at with (1400, 250, 140, 24) to platforms
    add platform at with (1840, 330, 120, 24) to platforms
    add platform at with (2400, 380, 60, 80) to platforms
    add platform at with (2640, 330, 60, 130) to platforms
    add coin at with (410, 320) to coins
    add coin at with (820, 380) to coins
    add coin at with (1250, 300) to coins
    add coin at with (1470, 210) to coins
    add coin at with (1900, 290) to coins
    add coin at with (2430, 340) to coins
    add coin at with (2670, 290) to coins
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 43
zoom: lines 36-43
spot: lines 36-43
say:
  Platform at is a small helper. It conjures a platform, sets its four parts, and returns it. Coin at does the same for a coin.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
line: 61
zoom: lines 51-61
spot: lines 52-55
say:
  Build the level adds every platform. These four are the ground, four hundred and sixty dots down, with gaps between them to fall into. The whole level is over three thousand dots wide, much wider than the window.
spot: lines 56-61
say:
  And these are ledges floating in the air, and two pillars to jump over.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
line: 69
zoom: lines 62-69
say:
  And seven coins, each floating where the player will have to jump to reach it.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  whisper: ---- moving the player: speed changes the place, gravity changes the speed

  
type fast:
  to run and jump:
    if key held "left":
      set speed x to speed x minus 0.8
      set facing to -1
    otherwise if key held "right":
      set speed x to speed x plus 0.8
      set facing to 1
    otherwise:
      set speed x to speed x times 0.8
    set speed x to clamp speed x between -6 and 6
    if key pressed "space" and on ground:
      set speed y to -13
    set speed y to smaller of (speed y plus 0.6) and 14
  and the universe said i love you
keys: enter
say:
  This is the most important part of the game. Let's go through it slowly.
line: 86
zoom: lines 73-86
spot: lines 74-76
say:
  If the left key is held, speed x goes down by point eight each frame, so the player speeds up toward the left. Not all at once, but a little more every frame. That's what makes running feel smooth.
spot: lines 77-79
say:
  The right key does the same toward the right.
spot: lines 80-81
say:
  With neither key held, speed x is multiplied by point eight every frame. So the player slows down, and glides to a stop, like friction.
spot: line 82
say:
  Clamp keeps speed x between minus six and six, so the player can't run faster than six dots a frame.
spot: lines 83-84
say:
  Jumping. Key pressed is yes for only one frame per press. And only on the ground: no jumping in mid air. A jump sets speed y to minus thirteen. On a screen, up is less, so minus thirteen means thirteen dots up, every frame.
spot: line 85
say:
  And this one line is gravity. Every frame, speed y grows by point six. So a jump starts fast upward, slows, stops at the top, and comes back down faster and faster, in a smooth curve. Smaller of keeps the fall from getting faster than fourteen.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  to overlaps, given p (a platform), returning yes or no:
    return player x plus 30 exceeds the x of p and player x falls below the x of p plus the width of p and player y plus 40 exceeds the y of p and player y falls below the y of p plus the height of p
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 90
zoom: lines 88-90
spot: lines 88-89
say:
  Overlaps asks whether the player's box and a platform's box overlap. The player is thirty dots wide and forty high. Two boxes overlap when each one's left edge is left of the other's right edge, and each one's top is above the other's bottom. All four must be true.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  to move across:
    set player x to player x plus speed x
    for each p in platforms:
      if overlaps with p:
        if speed x exceeds 0: set player x to the x of p minus 30
        otherwise: set player x to the x of p plus the width of p
        set speed x to 0
    set player x to clamp player x between 0 and 3170
  and the universe said i love you

  to move up and down:
    set on ground to no
    set player y to player y plus speed y
    for each p in platforms:
      if overlaps with p:
        if speed y exceeds 0:
          set player y to the y of p minus 40
          set on ground to yes
        otherwise:
          set player y to the y of p plus the height of p
        set speed y to 0
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 100
zoom: lines 92-100
spot: line 93
say:
  Now the moving itself. The trick is to move across and down separately, one after the other. First across: add speed x to player x.
spot: lines 94-98
say:
  Then, if that put the player inside a platform, push it back out. Moving right, the player's right side goes against the platform's left edge. Moving left, its left side goes against the platform's right edge. And speed x becomes zero: we hit a wall.
spot: line 99
say:
  And clamp keeps the player inside the level, from its left end to its right end.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
line: 112
zoom: lines 102-112
spot: lines 103-104
say:
  Then up and down, the same way. On ground starts as no, and speed y moves the player.
spot: lines 107-109
say:
  If the player was falling into a platform, its feet are put on the platform's top, and now, on ground is yes. That's the only place on ground ever becomes yes, so jumping only works after landing.
spot: lines 110-111
say:
  And if it was going up, it bumped its head: it's put just under the platform.
spot: line 112
say:
  Either way, speed y becomes zero.
say:
  Why separately? If we moved both at once, and ended up inside a corner of a platform, we couldn't tell whether we hit a wall or landed on a floor. One direction at a time, the answer is always clear.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  to pick up coins:
    set middle to (player x plus 15) horizontal (player y plus 20) vertical
    for each n from 1 to the size of coins:
      if not the taken of item n of coins and the existence of item n of coins is within 30 of middle:
        set the taken of item n of coins to yes
        set score to score plus 1
  and the universe said i love you

  to start again:
    set player x to 60
    set player y to 400
    set speed x to 0
    set speed y to 0
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 121
zoom: lines 115-121
spot: line 116
say:
  Picking up coins. Middle is the middle of the player: fifteen across and twenty down from its corner.
spot: lines 118-120
say:
  Any coin not taken yet, within thirty dots of the middle, is taken, and the score goes up. Setting the taken of item n of coins changes the coin inside the list itself.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
line: 128
zoom: lines 123-128
spot: lines 124-127
say:
  And start again puts the player back at the beginning, standing still. It's used at the start, and after falling into a gap.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  whisper: ---- drawing: the camera follows the player, and everything is etched where the camera sees it

  to etch the sky:
    consume the window with "#8fd3ff"
    for each n from 0 to 6:
      set hill x to n times 520 minus remainder of (camera times 0.3, 520)
      etch a circle with hill x, 560, 260, "#7fc8a9"
    for each n from 0 to 8:
      set cloud x to n times 400 minus remainder of (camera times 0.15, 400)
      etch a circle with cloud x, 90 plus (remainder of (n, 3)) times 25, 26, "white"
      etch a circle with cloud x plus 30, 80 plus (remainder of (n, 3)) times 25, 32, "white"
      etch a circle with cloud x plus 62, 92 plus (remainder of (n, 3)) times 25, 24, "white"
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 142
zoom: lines 132-142
spot: line 133
say:
  Now the drawing. Every frame, the whole picture is drawn again from the back to the front. First, consume the window fills it with sky blue.
spot: lines 134-136
say:
  Then hills. Here's the camera's first trick. The hills move at only point three of the camera's speed, so they seem far away. Remainder of wraps them around, so they never run out.
spot: lines 137-141
say:
  Clouds move even slower, at point one five, so they seem further still. Each cloud is three white circles. Things further away moving slower is called parallax, and it makes a flat game look deep.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  to etch the level:
    for each p in platforms:
      set left to the x of p minus camera
      etch a box with left, the y of p, the width of p, the height of p, "#8a5a33"
      etch a box with left, the y of p, the width of p, 10, "#4caf50"
    for each c in coins:
      if not the taken of c:
        set bob to sine of (ticks times 4 plus (the existence of c of horizontal)) times 4
        etch a circle with (the existence of c of horizontal) minus camera, (the existence of c of vertical) plus bob, 11, "#ffc107"
        etch a circle with (the existence of c of horizontal) minus camera minus 3, (the existence of c of vertical) plus bob minus 3, 4, "#fff3c4"
    etch a box with 3060 minus camera, 300, 6, 160, "#5d4037"
    set flag colour to "#e53935"
    if score is the size of coins: set flag colour to "#43a047"
    etch a box with 3066 minus camera, 300, 46, 30, flag colour
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 158
zoom: lines 144-158
spot: lines 145-148
say:
  The level. Here's the camera's main trick. Every platform is drawn at its x minus camera. As the player runs right, the camera grows, and everything slides left. The level never moves: only the view of it does. Each platform is brown earth with a strip of green grass on top.
spot: lines 149-153
say:
  Coins not yet taken are drawn as gold circles with a shine. Bob is sine of time, times four, so each coin floats gently up and down. Adding the coin's own x makes each one bob a little out of step with the others.
spot: lines 154-157
say:
  And the flag at the end turns from red to green once every coin is taken.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  to etch the player:
    set left to player x minus camera
    set squash to 0
    if not on ground: set squash to 4
    etch a box with left, player y plus squash, 30, 40 minus squash, "#3f51b5"
    set eye x to left plus 15 plus facing times 6
    etch a circle with eye x minus 5, player y plus 14, 4, "white"
    etch a circle with eye x plus 5, player y plus 14, 4, "white"
    etch a circle with eye x minus 5 plus facing, player y plus 14, 2, "black"
    etch a circle with eye x plus 5 plus facing, player y plus 14, 2, "black"
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 170
zoom: lines 160-170
spot: lines 162-164
say:
  The player is a blue box. In the air, squash makes it four dots shorter, which makes jumps feel lively.
spot: lines 165-169
say:
  And two eyes, moved six dots toward the way it's facing, with the pupils one more. Small things like this give a block a personality.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  to etch the score:
    set the text size to 26
    etch words with "Coins: [score] of [the size of coins]", 22, 18, "#1b2a49"
    if won:
      set the text size to 64
      etch words with "You win!", 340, 180, "#1b2a49"
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 178
zoom: lines 172-178
spot: lines 173-177
say:
  And the score at the top. Once the game is won, it says so, in big letters.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  genesis:
    open a world with "Hop!", 960, 540
    build the level
    start again
    set facing to 1
    as long as the world lives:
      if not won:
        run and jump
        move across
        move up and down
        pick up coins
        if player y exceeds 700: start again
        if score is the size of coins and player x exceeds 3030: set won to yes
      set camera to clamp (player x minus 360) between 0 and 2240
      set ticks to ticks plus frame time
      etch the sky
      etch the level
      etch the player
      etch the score
      reveal the frame
  and the universe said i love you
line: 200
zoom: lines 180-200
spot: lines 181-183
say:
  Last, genesis. It opens a window nine hundred and sixty by five hundred and forty, builds the level, and puts the player at the start.
spot: line 185
say:
  As long as the world lives repeats once a frame, sixty times a second, until the window is closed. Everything inside it is one frame of the game.
spot: lines 186-190
say:
  While the game isn't won: read the keys, move across, move up and down, and pick up coins. In that order, every frame.
spot: line 191
say:
  Fall below the screen, and you start again.
spot: line 192
say:
  Every coin, and past the flag: you've won.
spot: line 193
say:
  The camera follows the player, keeping it three hundred and sixty dots from the left of the window. Clamp stops it at the level's two ends, so we never see past them.
spot: lines 195-199
say:
  Then draw it all, back to front: sky, level, player, score. And reveal the frame shows the finished picture, and waits, so there are sixty frames a second.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
zoom: out
say:
  That's the whole game, two hundred lines. Let's play it.
game: hop.stella | hop.keys | Hop!
say:
  Running right, faster each frame. Jumping: up fast, slowing, and falling, as gravity adds point six every frame.
say:
  Coins, ledges, pillars, and the flag turns green. We win!
until game ends
say:
  And that's how a platform game works: speed changes the place, gravity changes the speed, one direction at a time, and a camera that the whole world is drawn around.
card: Thank you | That's Stellanova | Every word is in the list on the left of the studio, and in LANGUAGE.md. Now go and make something of your own.
say:
  That's every part of Stellanova. Every word is listed in the studio, and in the language guide. Now it's your turn: go and make something of your own.
pause: 2
